2.explanation:
• LabelUtil.isLabelExternal: this function is responsible for marking bpmn:MessageFlow elements as having an external label. If this list is wrong or incomplete, the importer will never attempt to render a label for the message icon.

• BpmnImporter.prototype.add: after creating a connection element for a MessageFlow, this method checks isLabelExternal(semantic) and then calls addLabel only if getLabel(element) returns non-empty text. Since getLabel currently only reads the flow’s own name property (and not the referenced <bpmn:Message> name), it never returns the underlying message name.

• BpmnImporter.prototype.addLabel: this is the routine that actually creates and positions the label shape under the connection. Even if LabelUtil and getLabel returned a string, any miscalculation of bounds or a missing call here would suppress the text.

• BpmnRenderer.prototype.drawConnection: this renderer hook draws the MessageFlow line and the envelope marker. If a label shape has been added by the importer, drawConnection (or drawShape in the renderer) must still delegate to the TextRenderer to actually draw the label text. If it intentionally skips label rendering for MessageFlow or for shapes of type ‘label’ attached to message flows, the text will not appear.

To fix the problem, one must
  • enhance getLabel (in features/label-editing/LabelUtil.js) to return semantic.messageRef.name when semantic.$type === 'bpmn:MessageFlow' and no other name is set;
  • verify that isLabelExternal indeed returns true for MessageFlow;
  • ensure BpmnImporter.add invokes addLabel for MessageFlows when getLabel now returns the messageRef name;
  • and confirm that BpmnRenderer.drawConnection (or drawShape) does not filter out label shapes on MessageFlows so that the TextRenderer will actually paint the name under the envelope icon.
